NOW native endpoint prototype
=============================

Compatibility and opt-in
------------------------

NowProtoClientContext remains exactly { handle, custom, Write, Read }. Existing
consumers still receive that context through drdynvc's OnChannelConnected and
OnChannelDisconnected. Without opt-in, NOW still rejects a second simultaneous
offer and uses legacy Read/Write. A disconnected legacy Write now returns
ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED rather than dereferencing a freed callback.
The legacy context itself is still session-owned; legacy callers must stop
using it before native session teardown. Only retained V2 endpoints outlive it.

The new exported freerdp_nowproto_client_set_callbacks(context, callbacks) accepts
NowProtoClientCallbacksV2 from <freerdp/client/nowproto.h>. Set size to sizeof the
struct and version to NOWPROTO_CLIENT_CALLBACKS_VERSION_2. All three callbacks
(Offer, Receive, Closed) are required. The table is copied and frozen. Install it
before the first channel opens; the first NOW OnChannelConnected callback is an
appropriate interop attachment point. After opt-in legacy Write explicitly returns
ERROR_INVALID_STATE and legacy Read is no longer used.

V2 ABI:

  NowProtoClientCallbacksV2:
    UINT32 size, version;
    void* custom;
    void Offer(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);
    void Receive(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint,
                 const BYTE* data, size_t size);
    void Closed(void* custom, NowProtoEndpoint* endpoint);

  NowProtoEndpoint (function pointers, each taking endpoint first):
    void Retain(endpoint);
    void Release(endpoint);
    UINT Write(endpoint, const BYTE* data, UINT32 size);
    UINT Close(endpoint);

These are native C ABI types (including native size_t and pointer alignment).
Managed bindings must root their delegates and custom state until native session
teardown returns. No NativeSessions or RDM binding is included in this prototype.

Offer and lifetime contract
---------------------------

Each accepted offer gets a distinct refcounted endpoint. V2 accepts overlapping
offers but does not choose a winner or retire the predecessor automatically.
Offer runs on OnOpen, after the CREATE response, not while the channel is in
INIT. Receive and Closed always carry that same endpoint identity. Closed runs
once per offered endpoint, for local close, remote close, or session teardown.
The listener-level drdynvc interface deliberately stays the legacy context;
consumers must use V2 events, not infer endpoint identity from that interface.

Callback endpoints and receive buffers are borrowed. Retain the endpoint and
copy receive bytes before enqueueing asynchronous work. Release every retained
reference, even after Closed or session teardown. Operations need an owned
reference; racing the release of one's final reference against a new operation
is a consumer error. Internal callback/operation references permit a consumer to
release its own reference from a reentrant callback.

Callbacks are synchronous, may run on different native threads, and must only
copy/enqueue work. They must never wait for managed asynchronous work, join a
thread that is calling an endpoint, or synchronously destroy the RDP session.
Close may invoke Closed inside Offer or Receive. No NOW plugin lock is held
during user callbacks, but drdynvc's recursive channel-table lock is held.

Write submits/copies nonempty bytes synchronously and returns the transport's
status. It rejects null/empty input with ERROR_INVALID_PARAMETER and a retired
endpoint with ERROR_NOT_CONNECTED. Close is idempotent, targets only its offer,
and does not await a remote close acknowledgement. Its first call reports a
close-PDU send failure but still retires the endpoint locally; subsequent calls
are successful no-ops. A stale Close is a no-op,
never a request against a newer offer, even when the server reuses a channel ID.
No native negotiation, admission policy, queue, timeout, or generation-publication
logic is added.

Transport safety
----------------

NOW uses its own listener rather than changing generic_dynvc's single-current
callback policy. A small opt-in drdynvc transport lease is necessary because a
raw IWTSVirtualChannel pointer and its channel ID do not provide lifetime safety.
The lease serializes operations with receive/remote-close/table teardown using
the existing channelsById lock, pins the native channel during an operation,
and becomes inert when that specific channel is freed. Retained leases keep
only the empty channel table/lock alive after manager destruction, not the
session or transport. During table teardown all lease operations are disabled,
including reentrant cross-channel close from Closed.

OnOpen also takes the table lock and a channel reference so that synchronous
Close from Offer cannot free its caller's channel. No public IWTS* structure
layout is changed. Other generic DVC clients do not opt into leases.

Validation
----------

Use the repository's internal test mode (BUILD_TESTING=OFF,
BUILD_TESTING_INTERNAL=ON), with WITH_CLIENT_COMMON and CHANNEL_NOWPROTO_CLIENT.
Build targets TestNowProto and TestDrdynvc, then run:

  ctest --test-dir <build> -R 'Test(NowProtoLifetime|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' \
    --output-on-failure

TestNowProtoLifetime exercises the actual drdynvc PDU path with an in-process
static-channel transport: legacy compatibility, endpoint routing, overlapping
offers, reused wire IDs, stale/idempotent close, reentrant Offer/Receive/Closed
with reference release, write versus remote/local close, and write versus
session teardown with endpoints retained afterwards. Forced transport barriers
exercise actual lock contention; injected write/close failures verify statuses.

This is not a Windows/macOS/Linux live-server interoperability or RDM supervision
parity test. Real network/server shutdown behavior still needs integration
coverage. The opt-in lease path preserves close-PDU errors that drdynvc's legacy
callback result precedence otherwise hides; generic clients retain that legacy
behavior.

Prototype validation on macOS arm64:

  cmake --build build/now-native --target TestDrdynvc TestNowProto --parallel 4
  ctest --test-dir build/now-native \
    -R 'Test(NowProtoLifetime|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' --repeat until-fail:50 \
    --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Both focused tests passed 50 repetitions each. The build uses minimal-library,
Debug, WITH_CLIENT_COMMON=ON, BUILD_TESTING=OFF, BUILD_TESTING_INTERNAL=ON,
ENABLE_WARNING_VERBOSE=OFF, CHANNEL_RDPECAM_CLIENT=OFF (no libv4l on macOS).

  ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=0 UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-asan \
      -R 'Test(NowProtoLifetime|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' --repeat until-fail:20 \
      --output-on-failure --timeout 60

Both tests passed 20 repetitions each with -fsanitize=address,undefined and
-fno-omit-frame-pointer. LeakSanitizer is disabled on macOS. This configuration
uses WITH_UNICODE_BUILTIN=OFF: the initial built-in converter run stopped in
pre-existing unicode_builtin.c:246 null-pointer arithmetic, also reproduced by
TestDrdynvcUnknownChannel before any NOW use.

ThreadSanitizer validation is NOT clean: -fsanitize=thread first stops in the
existing freerdp_timer_free/timer_thread race. Suppressing only that baseline
race exposes WinPR's critical.c:193/243 OwningThread read/write race during
real lock contention. Neither baseline issue is changed or broadly suppressed
by this prototype. Forced-race functional tests and ASan/UBSan pass, but these
do not establish a TSan-clean runtime or production interoperability.

  TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1 \
    ctest --test-dir build/now-native-tsan \
      -R 'Test(NowProtoLifetime|DrdynvcUnknownChannel)$' --output-on-failure

The diagnostic follow-up used a temporary suppression containing only
"race:freerdp_timer_free"; it still failed on WinPR lock metadata, not on NOW
endpoint state. Because it stops there, full NOW race coverage remains
unverified. Build and validation logs are retained in each build directory.
